WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.748 --> 00:00:09.469
Narrator: Face it, restrooms are gross.

00:00:09.469 --> 00:00:13.825
And public bathrooms are especially gross.

00:00:13.825 --> 00:00:16.967
After all, they don't have
lids on their toilet seats,

00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:20.648
so when you flush, it
launches a spray of bacteria

00:00:20.648 --> 00:00:24.799
nearly 2 meters away,
onto the stall walls,

00:00:24.799 --> 00:00:28.303
onto the door handle,
the seat, everywhere.

00:00:28.303 --> 00:00:31.622
Now, most of these bacteria are harmless.

00:00:31.622 --> 00:00:34.780
But a few can lead to nasty infections.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.181
For example, salmonella and E. coli,

00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:40.205
which can stow away in stool

00:00:40.205 --> 00:00:42.851
and can trigger horrible diarrhea.

00:00:42.851 --> 00:00:45.125
So, beyond swearing off public restrooms

00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:49.030
for the rest of your life,
how do you stay clean?

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.731
You can wash away germs,
which might make you sick.

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:57.640
Well, the obvious answer
is to wash your hands.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.738
Sounds easy enough, right?

00:01:00.738 --> 00:01:04.298
Well, the sad fact is
that many people don't.

00:01:04.298 --> 00:01:06.259
One-third of Americans admit

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:09.353
that they don't wash up
after using the bathroom.

00:01:09.353 --> 00:01:11.836
And that's just the ones who admit it!

00:01:11.836 --> 00:01:14.508
And even of the ones who do wash,

00:01:14.508 --> 00:01:17.357
many don't wash the right way.

00:01:17.357 --> 00:01:20.748
The CDC recommends that
you wash for 20 seconds.

00:01:20.748 --> 00:01:24.038
That's about as long as one ad on YouTube.

00:01:24.038 --> 00:01:25.776
You can manage it.

00:01:25.776 --> 00:01:28.763
But about 60% of Americans reportedly wash

00:01:28.763 --> 00:01:31.939
for only 15 seconds or less.

00:01:31.939 --> 00:01:35.521
And when you do wash, you
should wash with soap.

00:01:35.521 --> 00:01:39.175
It's way more effective than
using just plain old water.

00:01:40.092 --> 00:01:43.547
In a 2011 study, three
groups of volunteers

00:01:43.547 --> 00:01:46.235
were examined after touching door handles

00:01:46.235 --> 00:01:49.789
and hand railings in public spaces.

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:52.436
The first group didn't wash at all,

00:01:52.436 --> 00:01:54.687
the second washed with plain water,

00:01:54.687 --> 00:01:58.072
and the third washed with soap and water.

00:01:58.072 --> 00:02:00.397
And the results were telling.

00:02:00.397 --> 00:02:02.383
The researchers found fecal bacteria

00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:05.912
in 44% of the no-wash samples,

00:02:05.912 --> 00:02:09.102
23% of the only-water samples,

00:02:09.102 --> 00:02:12.798
and a mere 8% of the soap samples.

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:15.879
But, unfortunately, 70% of Americans

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:18.590
reportedly skip the soap.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:19.964
Yikes.

00:02:19.964 --> 00:02:24.104
To make matters worse, soap is
where things get complicated.

00:02:24.104 --> 00:02:27.937
Because, as it turns out, not
all soap is created equal.

00:02:27.937 --> 00:02:30.534
While your run-of-the-mill
soap can't hurt,

00:02:30.534 --> 00:02:35.003
antibacterial soap comes
with a nasty side effect.

00:02:35.003 --> 00:02:36.933
You see, your hands are normally covered

00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:39.500
with harmless, native bacteria,

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.716
some of which can
actually keep you healthy

00:02:41.716 --> 00:02:44.452
by fighting off harmful germs.

00:02:44.452 --> 00:02:49.210
But antibacterial soap can
kill off those native species,

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.699
leaving you more susceptible
to a nasty infection.

00:02:52.699 --> 00:02:54.323
So, your best bet is to avoid

00:02:54.323 --> 00:02:57.453
the antibacterial kind whenever possible.

00:02:57.453 --> 00:02:59.824
Of course, that's easier said than done

00:02:59.824 --> 00:03:03.016
in a public restroom, where
you don't have much control

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:05.265
over the type of soap that's available.

00:03:05.265 --> 00:03:08.634
But no soap at all isn't
a great option, either.

00:03:08.634 --> 00:03:12.742
So, clearly, it's time to
start a global movement.

00:03:12.742 --> 00:03:15.070
Hashtag #BYOS.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.441
Or, bring your own soap.

00:03:17.441 --> 00:03:18.466
Just kidding.

00:03:18.466 --> 00:03:21.283
That being said, no matter
what your soap situation

00:03:21.283 --> 00:03:23.247
might be, you're still better off

00:03:23.247 --> 00:03:26.303
using a public restroom
than holding it in.

00:03:26.303 --> 00:03:29.557
Because while the occasional
potty dance won't hurt you,

00:03:29.557 --> 00:03:31.961
building a habit of holding
it in for long periods

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:33.394
can stretch out the muscles

00:03:33.394 --> 00:03:35.838
you use to control your bladder.

00:03:35.838 --> 00:03:39.739
And over many, many years,
this can lead to leaking

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:42.784
or even infections and other issues.

00:03:42.784 --> 00:03:44.430
So it's probably worth it

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.440
to brave that stadium
restroom at halftime.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.513
Just, you know, wash your hands afterward.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.203
Please?

